  "question": {
    "text": "Let $L$ be the line of intersection of the planes $2 x+3 y+z=1$ and $x+3 y+2 z=2$. If $L$ makes an angles $\\alpha$ with the positive $x$-axis, then $\\cos \\alpha$ equals",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "4SiYT",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "QwS64",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "nIWmd",
      "text": "\n$1$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "xagPT",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If direction cosines of $L$ be $l$, $m, n$, then\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& 2 l+3 m+n=0 \\\\\n& l+3 m+2 n=0\n\\end{aligned}$\nSolving, we get, $\\frac{l}{3}=\\frac{m}{-3}=\\frac{n}{3}$\n$\\therefore l: m: n=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}:-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}: \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\Rightarrow \\cos \\alpha=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\text {. }$",
    "image": null
  },
